{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-5-20251101","id":"msg_01PQXRQbd29DZgB26YTwH3zX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Let me check the logic more carefully. Looking at the data:\n- T002: effective_date 2024-03-02 > biz_date 2024-03-01 - correct as AFTER_BIZ_DATE\n- T012: effective_date 2024-03-05 > biz_date 2024-03-01 - correct as AFTER_BIZ_DATE\n\nBut there's an issue - T002 has effective_date 2024-03-02 but the issue is the FX_MISSING check should come before AFTER_BIZ_DATE. Let me re-read the requirements:\n\nThe order of exception checks is:\n1. REVERSED (both rows if reversal_of points to any tx_id in file)\n2. REVERSAL_MISSING\n3. FX_MISSING (before FX - means before AFTER_BIZ_DATE since FX is used)\n4. AFTER_BIZ_DATE\n5. status!=POSTED\n6. DUPLICATE\n\nSo my order has FX_MISSING checked correctly. But wait, let me look at T002 more carefully: it's EUR to USD account. Let me verify the FX conversion is available.\n\nActually, looking at the code, the REVERSED check only applies if the tx has reversal_of OR if it is the target of a reversal. But T002 isn't reversed. Let me trace through:\n\nT002: A1001, 2024-03-02, -500 EUR, POSTED, no reversal_of\n- Not in reversedTxIds (no one is reversing T002)\n- reversal_of is empty\n- Account A1001 is USD, tx is EUR - need FX from EUR to USD on 2024-03-02\n- We have EUR->USD rate on 2024-03-01 (1.0870), which is <= 2024-03-02\n- So FX is available\n- effective_date 2024-03-02 > biz_date 2024-03-01 - AFTER_BIZ_DATE\n\nThat's correct! But let me verify other transactions.\n\nActually, looking at T016 row 17: A9999,2024-03-01,25.00,USD,POSTED - account A9999 doesn't exist. And row 18 is T016 again for A1001. So row 18 should be DUPLICATE.\n\nBut the issue is T016 row 17 has unknown account - should this be an exception? Looking at my code, I skip unknown account transactions but don't add them to exceptions. Let me re-examine the requirements - it says nothing about unknown accounts being exceptions. They should probably just be skipped silently (since they contribute nothing to the output).\n\nWait, I need to reconsider the exception logic. The order says \"in file order\" which means we process transactions in file order. Let me trace through each transaction again carefully:\n\nRow 1 (T001): A1001, 2024-03-01, -2000 USD, POSTED, no reversal\n- Not reversed by anyone (checking all reversal_of in file: T005, NOPE, T009, T018)\n- T005 reverses... nothing points to T001\n- Valid so far, let's continue checking\n\nWait, I need to find all reversal targets:\n- T006 reverses T005\n- T008 reverses NOPE (doesn't exist)\n- T010 reverses T009\n- T017 reverses T018\n\nSo reversedTxIds = {T005, T009, T018}\n\nAnd the transactions with reversal_of that point to valid tx_ids:\n- T006 (reversal_of=T005, T005 exists) -> both T005 and T006 are REVERSED\n- T010 (reversal_of=T009, T009 exists) -> both T009 and T010 are REVERSED  \n- T017 (reversal_of=T018, T018 exists) -> both T017 and T018 are REVERSED\n\nLet me trace through each row:\n\n1. T001: Not reversed, no reversal_of -> check FX (USD to USD = ok), date <= biz_date, POSTED -> VALID\n2. T002: Not reversed, no reversal_of -> EUR to USD FX exists, date 2024-03-02 > 2024-03-01 -> AFTER_BIZ_DATE\n3. T003: Not reversed, no reversal_of -> USD to USD, date 2024-02-28 <= 2024-03-01, status=PENDING -> PENDING\n4. T004: first occurrence, not reversed -> USD to USD, date 2024-02-15 <= 2024-03-01, POSTED -> VALID (add to seenTxIds)\n5. T004: second occurrence, seenTxIds contains T004 -> DUPLICATE\n6. T005: In reversedTxIds (T006 reverses it) -> REVERSED\n7. T006: reversal_of=T005, T005 exists -> REVERSED\n8. T007: A1003, USD to EUR needed (account is EUR), 2024-03-01... Let me check FX from USD to EUR\n9. T008: reversal_of=NOPE, NOPE not in allTxIds -> REVERSAL_MISSING\n10. T009: In reversedTxIds (T010 reverses it) -> REVERSED\n11. T010: reversal_of=T009, T009 exists -> REVERSED\n12. T011: A1002, USD to USD, 2024-03-01, POSTED -> VALID\n13. T012: date 2024-03-05 > 2024-03-01 -> AFTER_BIZ_DATE\n14. T013: A1001, GBP to USD, 2024-02-10... need FX GBP->USD\n15. T014: A1001, JPY to USD, 2024-03-01... need FX JPY->USD\n16. T015: A1002, CAD to USD, 2024-03-01... need FX CAD->USD\n17. T016: A9999 doesn't exist - skip (not an exception, just ignored)\n18. T016: second occurrence, seenTxIds contains T016 -> DUPLICATE\n19. T017: reversal_of=T018, T018 exists -> REVERSED\n20. T018: In reversedTxIds (T017 reverses it) -> REVERSED\n\nLooking at my output, row 2 is T002 with AFTER_BIZ_DATE - but should row 2 be checked? Let me verify row numbering:\n- Row 1: T001\n- Row 2: T002 -> AFTER_BIZ_DATE ✓\n- Row 3: T003 -> PENDING ✓\n- Row 5: T004 (duplicate) -> DUPLICATE ✓\n- Row 6: T005 -> REVERSED ✓\n- Row 7: T006 -> REVERSED ✓\n\nBut wait, my output says row 2 is T002, but doesn't show row 1 T001. That's correct since T001 is valid.\n\nHowever, the output shows:\n- Row 2, T002, AFTER_BIZ_DATE - but my logic says it should check FX_MISSING first\n\nLooking at the FX rates for EUR->USD on effective_date 2024-03-02:\n- Direct: EUR->USD on 2024-03-01 (rate 1.0870) - this is <= 2024-03-02, so it's valid\n\nSo FX is available, hence AFTER_BIZ_DATE is correct.\n\nBut wait, I see an issue. Row 4 (T004 first occurrence) should be valid, so rows 1 and 4 are valid. But my exceptions list shows row 5 as T004 DUPLICATE, which would be row 5 in the file (0-indexed row 4). Let me re-check my row numbering:\n\nIn my code: `t.rowNum = i + 1;` where i is the index in txRows (0-indexed after removing header).\n\nSo:\n- i=0 -> rowNum=1 (T001)\n- i=1 -> rowNum=2 (T002)\n- i=2 -> rowNum=3 (T003)\n- i=3 -> rowNum=4 (T004 first)\n- i=4 -> rowNum=5 (T004 second) -> DUPLICATE\n- i=5 -> rowNum=6 (T005) -> REVERSED\n- i=6 -> rowNum=7 (T006) -> REVERSED\n\nThis matches the output! The exceptions file shows row_num starting from 2 which is correct for 1-based data row numbering.\n\nBut wait, I notice an issue - my code treats T016 on row 17 (for A9999) as valid because it doesn't exist in accounts, so it just continues without adding to exceptions or validTxns. But then row 18 T016 (for A1001) gets marked as DUPLICATE because seenTxIds already contains T016 from row 17.\n\nActually looking more carefully - the first T016 is for A9999 which doesn't exist, so it just skips it. But seenTxIds.add(t.txId) happens regardless of whether the account exists. So T016 is added to seenTxIds on row 17, and row 18 is DUPLICATE.\n\nBut wait, the DUPLICATE check happens BEFORE we check if the account exists. So even if T016 row 17 has an invalid account, it still adds to seenTxIds, and row 18 gets DUPLICATE.\n\nLet me look at my code flow again:\n1. Check REVERSED\n2. Check REVERSAL_MISSING  \n3. Check FX_MISSING (needs account to exist)\n4. Check AFTER_BIZ_DATE\n5. Check status!=POSTED\n6. Check DUPLICATE\n7. Add to seenTxIds\n8. If any reason, add to exceptions\n9. Else if account doesn't exist, skip\n10. Else add to validTxns\n\nSo for T016 row 17:\n- Not reversed\n- No reversal_of\n- Account A9999 doesn't exist, so FX_MISSING check: `if (acc != null && ...)` - acc is null, so skip FX check\n- Date 2024-03-01 <= 2024-03-01, ok\n- Status POSTED, ok\n- seenTxIds doesn't contain T016 yet, so not DUPLICATE\n- seenTxIds.add(T016)\n- reason is null\n- Account doesn't exist (accounts.containsKey returns false), so continue without adding to validTxns\n\nFor T016 row 18:\n- Not reversed\n- No reversal_of\n- Account A1001 exists, USD currency, tx currency USD - no FX needed\n- Date ok\n- Status POSTED\n- seenTxIds contains T016 -> DUPLICATE\n\nThis matches the output.\n\nNow let me verify the interest calculations. Let me trace through the valid transactions:\n\nValid txs: T001, T004 (first), T007, T011, T013, T014, T015\n\nWait, is T007 valid? Let me check:\n- T007: A1003, 2024-03-01, 10.00 USD, POSTED, no reversal\n- Account A1003 currency is EUR\n- Need FX from USD to EUR on 2024-03-01\n- Direct USD->EUR: 2024-03-01 rate 0.9200\n- Valid!\n\nT013: A1001, 2024-02-10, 30.00 GBP\n- Account A1001 is USD\n- Need FX from GBP to USD on 2024-02-10\n- Direct GBP->USD: none\n- Reverse USD->GBP: 2024-02-01 (0.7900) - this is <= 2024-02-10\n- So rate = 1/0.7900 = 1.2658227848 (10dp)\n\nT014: A1001, 2024-03-01, -10000 JPY\n- Account A1001 is USD\n- Need FX from JPY to USD on 2024-03-01\n- Direct JPY->USD: 2024-02-01 (0.0067)\n- Reverse USD->JPY: 2024-03-01 (150.00)\n- Compare dates: JPY->USD is 2024-02-01, USD->JPY is 2024-03-01\n- 2024-03-01 > 2024-02-01, so use reverse: 1/150 = 0.0066666667 (10dp)\n\nT015: A1002, 2024-03-01, 100.00 CAD\n- Account A1002 is USD\n- Need FX from CAD to USD on 2024-03-01\n- Direct CAD->USD: none\n- Reverse USD->CAD: none\n- Try pivots: GBP, EUR\n- GBP pivot: CAD->GBP and GBP->USD\n  - CAD->GBP direct: 2024-02-01 (0.5800)\n  - CAD->GBP reverse (GBP->CAD): 2024-03-01 (1.7200) -> 1/1.7200 = 0.5813953488\n  - Use newer: 2024-03-01, so CAD->GBP = 0.5813953488\n  - GBP->USD direct: none\n  - GBP->USD reverse (USD->GBP): 2024-03-01 (0.8000) -> 1/0.8000 = 1.25\n  - CAD->USD via GBP = 0.5813953488 * 1.25 = 0.7267441860 (10dp)\n\nLet me now verify the EOD balance calculations.\n\nAccount A1001 (USD, fee_plan PLAN_A - DEBIT only):\n- start_balance = 10000.00\n- T001: -2000.00 USD, DEBIT (negative amount)\n  - Fee: flat 0.50 + 0.005 * 2000 = 0.50 + 10.00 = 10.50\n  - Capped to [0.25, 5.00] -> 5.00\n  - adjusted = -2000.00 - 5.00 = -2005.00\n- T013: 30.00 GBP -> USD = 30 * 1.2658227848 = 37.97 (rounded 10dp then 2dp? Let me check)\n  Actually the conversion is done at 10dp precision, then fees are applied.\n  - 30.00 * 1.2658227848 = 37.9746835440 (10dp) \n  - This is a CREDIT (positive), PLAN_A only applies to DEBIT, so no fee\n  - adjusted = 37.97 (rounded to 2dp)\n  \nWait, let me re-read my code. The amount is multiplied by rate and rounded to 10dp:\n```java\namount = amount.multiply(rate).setScale(10, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);\n```\n\nThen fee is applied and adjusted is rounded to 2dp:\n```java\namount = amount.subtract(fee).setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);\n```\n\nSo for T013:\n- amount = 30.00 * 1.2658227848 = 37.9746835440 (10dp precision)\n- PLAN_A applies to DEBIT only, 37.97... is positive (CREDIT), so no fee\n- adjusted = 37.9746835440.setScale(2, HALF_EVEN) = 37.97\n\nHmm, but looking at my code, the rounding to 2dp only happens when a fee is applied. Let me check:\n\n```java\nif (applyFee) {\n    BigDecimal fee = rule.flatFee.add(rule.pctFee.multiply(amount.abs()));\n    fee = fee.setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);\n    fee = fee.max(rule.minFee).min(rule.maxFee);\n    amount = amount.subtract(fee).setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);\n}\n```\n\nSo if no fee is applied, the amount is not rounded. Then when adding to balance:\n```java\nbal = bal.add(amount).setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);\n```\n\nThe balance is rounded to 2dp after each addition. So:\n- After T001: 10000.00 + (-2005.00) = 7995.00\n- After T013: 7995.00 + 37.9746835440 = 8032.9746835440 -> 8032.97\n\nWait, that's not matching. Let me recalculate T014:\n- T014: -10000 JPY to USD\n- rate = 0.0066666667 (1/150 rounded to 10dp)\n- amount = -10000 * 0.0066666667 = -66.6666670000 (10dp)\n- This is a DEBIT (negative), PLAN_A applies\n- fee = 0.50 + 0.005 * 66.6666670000 = 0.50 + 0.3333333350 = 0.8333333350 -> 0.83 (2dp)\n- fee capped: max(0.25, min(5.00, 0.83)) = 0.83\n- adjusted = -66.6666670000 - 0.83 = -67.4966670000 -> -67.50 (2dp)\n\nSo for A1001:\n- Start: 10000.00\n- T001: 10000.00 + (-2005.00) = 7995.00\n- T013: 7995.00 + 37.9746835440 = 8032.9746835440 -> 8032.97\n- T014: 8032.97 + (-67.50) = 7965.47\n\nThat matches! A1001 eod_balance = 7965.47 ✓\n\nAccount A1002 (USD, fee_plan PLAN_B - ALL):\n- start_balance = 5000.00\n- T004: 50.00 USD, POSTED (first occurrence)\n  - fee = 0.10 + 0.002 * 50 = 0.10 + 0.10 = 0.20\n  - capped: max(0.10, min(2.00, 0.20)) = 0.20\n  - adjusted = 50.00 - 0.20 = 49.80\n- T011: 5.00 USD\n  - fee = 0.10 + 0.002 * 5 = 0.10 + 0.01 = 0.11\n  - capped: max(0.10, min(2.00, 0.11)) = 0.11\n  - adjusted = 5.00 - 0.11 = 4.89\n- T015: 100.00 CAD to USD\n  - rate = 0.7267441860\n  - amount = 100 * 0.7267441860 = 72.6744186000 (10dp)\n  - fee = 0.10 + 0.002 * 72.6744186000 = 0.10 + 0.1453488372 = 0.2453488372 -> 0.25 (2dp)\n  - capped: max(0.10, min(2.00, 0.25)) = 0.25\n  - adjusted = 72.6744186000 - 0.25 = 72.4244186000 -> 72.42 (2dp)\n\nSo A1002:\n- Start: 5000.00\n- T004: 5000.00 + 49.80 = 5049.80\n- T011: 5049.80 + 4.89 = 5054.69\n- T015: 5054.69 + 72.42 = 5127.11\n\nThat matches! A1002 eod_balance = 5127.11 ✓\n\nAccount A1003 (EUR, fee_plan PLAN_A - DEBIT only):\n- start_balance = -10.00\n- T007: 10.00 USD to EUR\n  - rate = 0.9200 (USD->EUR on 2024-03-01)\n  - amount = 10 * 0.92 = 9.2000000000\n  - This is CREDIT (positive), PLAN_A applies to DEBIT only, no fee\n  - adjusted = 9.20 (not rounded in my code since no fee applied, but let me check...)\n\nActually wait, I need to re-read the requirements. It says \"adjusted = amount - fee, rounded Half-even 2dp\". This suggests adjusted should always be 2dp. Let me check if the final balance rounding handles this.\n\nIn my code:\n```java\nbal = bal.add(amount).setScale(2, RoundingMode.HALF_EVEN);\n```\n\nSo balance is rounded to 2dp after each transaction. So:\n- Start: -10.00\n- T007: -10.00 + 9.20 = -0.80\n\nThat matches! A1003 eod_balance = -0.80 ✓\n\nAccount A1004 (USD, fee_plan PLAN_C - CREDIT only):\n- start_balance = 36500.00\n- No valid transactions (T009 and T010 are REVERSED)\n\nSo A1004 eod_balance = 36500.00 ✓\n\nNow let's verify APR calculations:\n\nA1001:\n- opened_at = 2024-02-01\n- biz_date = 2024-03-01\n- days_since_opened = 29 days (Feb 1 to Mar 1)\n- promo_days = 29, so daysSinceOpened (29) < promoDays (29) is FALSE\n- So no promo_apr\n- eod = 7965.47 >= tier_threshold 5000 -> add tier_apr\n- apr = base_apr (0.01000) + tier_apr (0.00200) = 0.01200\n\nHmm wait, I need to verify daysBetween. From 2024-02-01 to 2024-03-01:\n- Feb 1 = day 0 (opened_at)\n- Feb 2 = day 1\n- ...\n- Feb 29 (2024 is leap year) = day 28\n- Mar 1 = day 29\n\nSo daysSinceOpened = 29. promo_days = 29. The condition is `daysSinceOpened < promo_days`, so 29 < 29 is false. No promo.\n\napr = 0.01000 + 0.00200 = 0.01200 ✓\n\nA1002:\n- opened_at = 2020-01-01\n- days_since_opened = huge number\n- promo_days = 0, so promo condition: `if (acc.promoDays > 0 ...)` is false\n- eod = 5127.11 >= tier_threshold 5000 -> add tier_apr\n- apr = 0.00800 + 0.00100 = 0.00900 ✓\n\nA1003:\n- eod = -0.80 < tier_threshold 10000 -> no tier_apr\n- promo_days = 0\n- apr = 0.02000 ✓\n\nA1004:\n- eod = 36500.00 < tier_threshold 999999 -> no tier_apr\n- promo_days = 0\n- apr = 0.01005 ✓\n\nNow interest:\n- A1001: principal = max(7965.47, 0) = 7965.47\n  - daily_interest_raw = 7965.47 * 0.012 / 365 = 0.2618539178 (10dp)\n  - daily_interest = 0.26 (2dp)\n- A1002: principal = 5127.11\n  - daily_interest_raw = 5127.11 * 0.009 / 365 = 0.1264292137 (10dp)\n  - daily_interest = 0.13 (2dp)\n- A1003: principal = max(-0.80, 0) = 0\n  - daily_interest_raw = 0\n  - daily_interest = 0.00\n- A1004: principal = 36500.00\n  - daily_interest_raw = 36500 * 0.01005 / 365 = 1.0050000000\n  - daily_interest = 1.01 (2dp)\n\nWait, the output shows A1004 daily_interest as 1.00, not 1.01. Let me recalculate:\n36500 * 0.01005 = 366.825\n366.825 / 365 = 1.0050000000\n\nRounded to 2dp: 1.01 (since 0.005 rounds up with HALF_EVEN when the digit before is even... wait, HALF_EVEN rounds to the nearest even. 1.005 -> the digit is 5, the preceding digit is 0, which is even, so round down to 1.00).\n\nActually HALF_EVEN (banker's rounding): when the value is exactly halfway, round to the nearest even number. 1.005 -> 1.00 or 1.01? The digit before the 5 is 0, which is even, so we keep it as 1.00.\n\nThat's correct! A1004 daily_interest = 1.00 ✓\n\ntotal_interest_raw = 0.2618539178 + 0.1264292137 + 0 + 1.0050000000 = 1.3932831315\ntotal_interest = 1.39